{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789502115,"nonce":"ZTcHMFKnDErdCp6AebxBu/vGHwU=","title":"fannkuch: a bound method as a value is called through the dynamic path","message":"fannkuch binds `perm1_ins = perm1.insert` and `perm1_pop = perm1.pop` once and calls them 362,880 times each in the hot loop. Since 6fcc27b the binding is a function record whose code is an adapter: a call goes `zb_call_2(record, box(r), zb_call_1(pop_record, box(0)))`, the adapter unboxes each argument with a checked read, reaches the list through the record's cell, calls `zb_list_insert_i64`, and boxes the result; six boxes per iteration, all left to the collector. Measured on the M1 Pro: the kernel at n=9 takes 225 ms as written and 139 ms with the two calls spelled `perm1.insert(r, perm1.pop(0))`, so this is 86 ms of the 225 (PyPy runs the kernel in 86 ms).\n\nThe typer knows the receiver's type at the binding. A local bound once to `list.method` (never reassigned, never escaping as a value) should keep that knowledge, `Ty::Bound(receiver, method)`, so a call through it lowers as the direct method call, and the record is only made if the value escapes. The same for a bound method of an instance.","files":null}]}